\vec{v'} = \vec{v} - 2 \frac{\vec{v} \cdot \vec{n}}{\vec{n} \cdot \vec{n}} \vec{n}
\] where $\vec{n}$ is a normal vector to the line. For simplicity, we can assume the line passes through the origin, and the normal vector can be taken as $\vec{n} = \begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}$. Given that the reflection of $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ is $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$, we can use this information to find the normal vector. The direction vector of the line is then $\begin{pmatrix} -1 - 5 \\ 7 - (-5) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}$ which simplifies to $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$. The normal vector to this line is $\vec{n} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. The reflection formula then becomes: \[
\vec{v'} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{2x + y}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\] Applying this to the vector $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$, we get: \[
\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{2(-1) + 7}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{5}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 10 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}
\] This confirms our normal vector is correct. Now we can apply this reflection formula to the vector $\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$: \[
\vec{v'} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{2(-4) + 3}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} - 2

